====================================================================================================
VERDICT -- the audit Carl asked for
====================================================================================================
  Seven channels. Four CLOSED or negligible with the number/citation that says so (B by the
  corpus's own SN host-step null; D by the published disformal arc; E by cH(rec)/a0 ~ 2e5;
  F/G by order counting and no-mechanism). One OPEN as known (A, the unbuilt MI Friedmann
  calculation -- still the load-bearing item). And ONE GENUINELY MISSED (C):

  THE MISSED REFRAMING: the framework's own modest nu ~ 1.2-1.7 flow boost puts a coherent
  50-175 km/s into the low-z SN anchor, producing a 0.01-0.11 mag bias exactly where the
  phantom crossing lives (z_cross = 0.32-0.50 is fit-driven by the low-z lever arm). That is
  the SAME ORDER as the 0.025-0.069 mag signal (overlapping, not exceeding -- and diluted
  by sky-averaging in the actual fit). It needs NO forbidden sigma8 excess, so the
  closed bulk-flow door does not close it.

  SOBRIETY, before anyone gets excited:
   1. The SIGN is undetermined here. If it runs the wrong way it ARGUES AGAINST the
      artifact reading rather than for it.
   2. Novelty is thin: 'low-z SNe bias the DESI hint' exists WITHOUT modified inertia, and
      timescape/local-void own the mundane version. The framework adds an amplification
      factor and an a0-tied environmental signature, not the idea.
   3. Channel A remains the load-bearing calculation. C is a second, cheaper lever on the
      same target -- and unlike A it can be estimated from the framework's ALREADY-COMPUTED
      flow boost plus a public low-z SN compilation, with no new theory.

  ANSWER TO THE QUESTION 'did we do it all the way': NO. A was priced but not built, and C
  was missed entirely until this audit. Now both are on the record with their costs.
